    "collapsible-content": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "faq-0": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why doesn't this toothpaste foam?",
            "row_content": "<p>Foam comes from synthetic detergents like SLS, which is also a known canker-sore trigger for people prone to them. We left it out. Foaming isn't cleaning — it's a sensory cue you've been trained to expect. Your brush does the cleaning, and the lauric acid, baking soda, and myrrh do the protecting. After a week or two, most customers stop missing the foam.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why is the texture sometimes firmer or thinner?",
            "row_content": "<p>The texture shifts batch-to-batch because cold-pressed virgin coconut oil shifts batch-to-batch — every harvest has a slightly different fatty acid profile. Refined oil is uniform precisely because the lab strips that variation out, along with most of the lauric acid. We accept the inconsistency because the lauric acid is the antimicrobial. Shake before use; coconut oil firms up below 76°F.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Does this toothpaste contain fluoride?",
            "row_content": "<p>No fluoride. We formulated this for customers who prefer to avoid it. The cleaning and antimicrobial work is done by lauric acid from cold-pressed virgin coconut oil, pH balancing from aluminum-free baking soda, and sesquiterpenes from wildcrafted myrrh — independent of fluoride. If you want both, you can pair our paste with a fluoride rinse.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-2a": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is it safe to swallow? Safe for kids?",
            "row_content": "<p>Every ingredient is food-grade or food-derived: organic virgin coconut oil, aluminum-free baking soda, xanthan gum, wildcrafted myrrh, organic stevia, organic essential oils, purified spring water. With no fluoride in the tube, accidental swallowing isn't the same concern it is with conventional paste. No SLS detergent, no synthetic sweeteners, no artificial flavors.<\/p><p>That said, essential oils are concentrated. We still recommend rinsing and spitting like any toothpaste, and supervising young kids until they can spit reliably. If your child is in the toddler-first-toothpaste stage, ask your pediatric dentist about fluoride needs based on your local water and your child's risk profile.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is this safe for sensitive teeth and gums?",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes — it's what we built it for. No SLS (a known canker-sore trigger). No hydrated silica (harder than enamel; wears it down over years). No synthetic flavor amplifiers. Baking soda is softer than enamel, and wildcrafted myrrh's sesquiterpenes are anti-inflammatory and particularly soothing for irritated gum tissue. The customer with reactive gums is the customer we designed around.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why is the mint flavor so mild?",
            "row_content": "<p>The mint here is essential-oil mild — organic peppermint and spearmint, not synthetic flavor amplifiers. If you're used to industrial toothpaste, the first few brushings will feel quiet. Many customers come to prefer it. The Cinnamon Spice variation is more assertive if you want a stronger hit, because cinnamon essential oil is naturally stronger than mint.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How is this different from other natural toothpastes?",
            "row_content": "<p>\"Natural\" is essentially unregulated, and a lot of it isn't very. Read any natural toothpaste's ingredient list against ours and you'll typically find one or more of:<\/p><ul><li><strong>A foaming agent<\/strong> — SLS, sodium lauroyl sarcosinate, or sodium cocoyl glutamate. We use none. Foam doesn't clean, and SLS is a known canker-sore trigger.<\/li><li><strong>An abrasive harder than enamel<\/strong> — hydrated silica, calcium carbonate, or charcoal. We use baking soda, which is softer than enamel and adds pH balancing.<\/li><li><strong>A tooth-coating thickener<\/strong> — glycerin or sorbitol. We use xanthan gum, which gives the paste structure without coating teeth.<\/li><li><strong>Titanium dioxide<\/strong> for the bright-white look. We leave the paste the color of its actual ingredients.<\/li><li><strong>Hydroxyapatite<\/strong> as a marketing-friendly remineralizer. We rely on baking soda neutralizing oral acid and saliva doing what saliva does.<\/li><\/ul><p>Our list is shorter and we name what's missing as plainly as what's present. The wildcrafted myrrh in particular — most natural brands skip it because consistent sourcing is hard to scale.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How do I use it?",
            "row_content": "<p>Shake the bottle before use — coconut oil firms up below 76°F, so the gel can settle. Squeeze a pea-sized amount onto a wet toothbrush and brush as you normally would for two minutes. It won't foam. The mint will feel quieter than industrial paste. Many customers report teeth feel polished after use — a side effect of the lauric acid plus baking soda doing their work.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "faq-0",
